## In 30 seconds

JRebirth animates page switches with **ShowFadingModelCommand** (core): show model → cross-fade → detach old node. Use `appendChild(false)` on `DisplayModelWaveBean`. The optional **transition** module slices nodes into tiles for splash effects (`NodeSlicerCommand`, `SlidingDoorService`, `RandomFadingService`).

Quick Start
=========================

Model navigation transitions ship with **core** (no extra dependency). Advanced tile-slicer effects require the **transition** module.

```xml
<!-- Core transitions (ShowFadingModelCommand) — already on classpath with core -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>core</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>

<!-- Optional: node slicer transitions (SlidingDoorService, RandomFadingService) -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.jrebirth.af</groupId>
    <artifactId>transition</artifactId>
    <version>${jrebirth.version}</version>
</dependency>
```

**Minimal example — fade between two pages in a StackPane:**

```java
// StackModel calls this internally; you can also trigger it directly:
DisplayModelWaveBean waveBean = DisplayModelWaveBean.create()
    .childrenPlaceHolder(stackPane.getChildren())
    .appendChild(false)   // insert new node behind old one for cross-fade
    .showModelKey(Key.create(NewPageModel.class))
    .hideModelKey(currentModelKey);

callCommand(ShowFadingModelCommand.class, waveBean);
```

**Minimal example — sliding-door reveal (transition module):**

```java
@Override
protected void initInnerComponents() {
    NodeSlicerCommand slicer = getService(NodeSlicerCommand.class, "Splash");
    slicer.setImage(Resources.create(new RelImage("splash.png")).get());
    slicer.setTileWidth(4);
    slicer.setTileHeight(600);
    slicer.sliceNode(null, null);

    SlidingDoorService door = getService(SlidingDoorService.class, "Splash");
    door.setNodes(slicer.getSlices());
    door.setNodeDelay(6);
    door.doIt();
    door.getFullTransition().play();
}
```

The transition module test `TransitionModel` wires slicer + both services in `initInnerComponents()`:

```java
imageSlicerService = getService(NodeSlicerCommand.class, "Properties");
imageSlicerService.setImage(Resources.create(new RelImage("Properties.png")).get());
imageSlicerService.setTileHeight(600);
imageSlicerService.setTileWidth(4);
imageSlicerService.sliceNode(null, null);

slidingDoorService = getService(SlidingDoorService.class, "Properties");
slidingDoorService.setNodeDelay(4);
slidingDoorService.setNodes(imageSlicerService.getSlices());
slidingDoorService.doIt();
```


Transitions Overview
=========================

JRebirth provides transition effects at two levels:

* **Model Navigation Transitions (Core)** — Production-ready fading transitions for switching displayed models within a container (e.g. `StackModel` page navigation).
* **Node Slicer Transitions (transition module)** — Experimental advanced visual effects that slice nodes into tile grids for creative animations.


Model Navigation Transitions (Core)
==========================================

The core module includes built-in commands for animating model switches. These are the recommended way to transition between pages or panels with smooth fading effects.

### ShowFadingModelCommand

A `MultiCommand` that orchestrates the full model-switch sequence:

1. `ShowModelCommand` — Prepares and attaches the new model's node.
2. `FadeTransitionCommand` — Runs a parallel fade (old node fades out, new node fades in).
3. `DetachModelCommand` — Removes the old node after the animation completes.


```java
// ShowFadingModelCommand — sequential MultiCommand (JIT)
@Override
protected List<UniqueKey<? extends Command>> defineSubCommand() {
    return Arrays.asList(
        getCommandKey(ShowModelCommand.class),
        getCommandKey(FadeTransitionCommand.class),
        getCommandKey(DetachModelCommand.class));
}
```

`ShowFadingModelCommand` chains three commands. Always pass `appendChild(false)` on the wave bean so the new node sits **behind** the old one during the cross-fade.

### FadeTransitionCommand

Handles the actual JavaFX animation. It creates a `ParallelTransition` with two `FadeTransition` instances:

* Old node: opacity 1.0 → 0.0 (600ms)
* New node: opacity 0.0 → 1.0 (600ms)

Once the animation completes, the old node is removed from the container.


```java
@RunInto(RunType.JAT)
public class FadeTransitionCommand extends AbstractSingleCommand<DisplayModelWaveBean> {

    @Override
    protected void perform(final Wave wave) {
        Node oldNode = waveBean(wave).hideModel() == null ? null : waveBean(wave).hideModel().node();
        final Node newNode = waveBean(wave).showModel() == null ? null : waveBean(wave).showModel().node();

        if (oldNode != null || newNode != null) {
            final ParallelTransition animation = new ParallelTransition();

            if (oldNode != null) {
                final FadeTransition ft = new FadeTransition(Duration.millis(600), oldNode);
                ft.setFromValue(1.0);
                ft.setToValue(0.0);
                animation.getChildren().add(ft);
            }
            if (newNode != null) {
                final FadeTransition ft = new FadeTransition(Duration.millis(600), newNode);
                ft.setFromValue(0.0);
                ft.setToValue(1.0);
                animation.getChildren().add(ft);
            }

            final Node oldNodeLink = oldNode;
            animation.setOnFinished(event -> {
                if (oldNodeLink != null) {
                    waveBean(wave).childrenPlaceHolder().remove(oldNodeLink);
                }
                waveBean(wave).showModel().doShowView(wave);
            });
            animation.playFromStart();
        }
    }
}
```

`@RunInto(RunType.JAT)` runs the animation on the JavaFX thread. On completion, the old node is removed from the stack and `doShowView` activates the new model.

### Using ShowFadingModelCommand

To trigger a fading model transition programmatically, build a `DisplayModelWaveBean` and call the command:

```java
DisplayModelWaveBean waveBean = DisplayModelWaveBean.create()
    .childrenPlaceHolder(stackPane.getChildren())
    .appendChild(false)
    .showModelKey(Key.create(NewPageModel.class))
    .hideModelKey(currentModelKey);

callCommand(ShowFadingModelCommand.class, waveBean);
```

The `StackModel` component uses this command internally whenever you navigate between pages — you get fading transitions for free when using `StackModel` with `PageEnum`.


Node Slicer Transitions (transition module)
=================================================

The `transition` module provides experimental services for advanced visual effects. These work by slicing a node (or image) into a grid of tiles and animating each tile independently.

### SlidingDoorService

Applies a staggered translate animation to a grid of node tiles, producing a "sliding door" reveal effect.


```java
// SlidingDoorService — staggered translate per tile
public void doIt() {
    final ParallelTransition parallel = new ParallelTransition();
    int i = 0;
    for (final Node node : this.nodes) {
        final TranslateTransition tt = new TranslateTransition();
        tt.setDelay(Duration.millis(i * getNodeDelay()));
        tt.setNode(node);
        tt.setByY(1000);
        tt.setDuration(getTranslateDuration());
        tt.setInterpolator(Interpolator.EASE_IN);
        parallel.getChildren().add(tt);
        i++;
    }
    this.fullTransition = new SequentialTransition(parallel);
}
// slidingDoorService.getFullTransition().play();
```

Each tile receives a staggered `TranslateTransition` based on `nodeDelay`. The result is wrapped in a `SequentialTransition` you play via `getFullTransition().play()`.

Configuration properties:

| Property | Description | Default |
|----------|-------------|---------|
| `nodeDelay` | Delay increment between each tile's animation start (ms) | 4 |
| `slidingType` | Direction of the slide (e.g. `FromBottomLeft`, `FromTopRight`) | `FromBottomLeft` |
| `translateDuration` | Duration of each tile's translate animation | 500ms |

Supported `SlidingType` values:

* `FromBottomLeft`, `FromBottomRight`, `FromTopLeft`, `FromTopRight`
* `FromLeftBottom`, `FromRightBottom`, `FromLeftTop`, `FromRightTop`
* `FromHorizontalMiddle`, `FromVerticalMiddle`

### RandomFadingService

Shuffles the tile order randomly and fades them in sequence using a `Timeline` with `KeyFrame` entries, creating a randomized reveal effect.


```java
public void doIt() {
    Collections.shuffle(this.nodes);
    final List<KeyFrame> keyFrames = new ArrayList<>();
    long d = 100;
    for (final Node node : this.nodes) {
        d += 1000;
        keyFrames.add(new KeyFrame(Duration.millis(d),
            new KeyValue(node.opacityProperty(), 1.0)));
    }
    this.timeline = new Timeline();
    timeline.getKeyFrames().addAll(keyFrames);
}
// After doIt(): timeline.play();
```

Tiles are shuffled, then a `Timeline` fades each tile's opacity in sequence. Call `doIt()` after `setNodes()`, then play `getTimeline()`.

### Using Node Slicer Transitions

The typical workflow for slicer transitions:

1. **Slice the node** — Use `NodeSlicerCommand` to split a source node into a grid of `ImageView` tiles.
2. **Configure a service** — Set up `SlidingDoorService` or `RandomFadingService` with your tile nodes.
3. **Play the transition** — Call `doIt()` on the service, then play the resulting transition.

```java
SlidingDoorService service = getService(SlidingDoorService.class);
service.setNodes(tileNodes);
service.setNodeDelay(6);
service.setSlidingType(SlidingType.FromTopLeft);
service.doIt();
service.getFullTransition().play();
```

These effects are best suited for splash screens, presentation transitions, or creative UI animations rather than standard page navigation.


Choosing the Right Transition
====================================

| Use Case | Recommended Approach |
|----------|---------------------|
| Page navigation in StackModel | `ShowFadingModelCommand` (automatic) |
| Custom model switching | `ShowFadingModelCommand` with `DisplayModelWaveBean` |
| Splash / reveal effects | `SlidingDoorService` or `RandomFadingService` |
| Standard JavaFX animations | Use JavaFX `Transition` API directly in your View |


